This calculates how \"shiny\" or \"matte\" each part of the wall is. Because it depends on `h` (the height), the areas that are physically \"lower\" in the texture will have a **higher roughness value** (closer to 1.0). In most lighting engines, high roughness means the surface doesn't catch as much light and appears flatter or \"duller.\" 

If your environment has any ambient lighting or specular highlights, these \"dimmer\" areas won't reflect that light as well, making them appear as dark blotches even though their base color is exactly the same as the rest of the wall.

### [b]2. Normal Mapping / Lighting Geometry[/b]
The shader uses a **Normal Map** calculation (the `view_grad` and `NORMAL` lines). Because this is a procedural heightmap, it calculates how light should bounce off the \"bumps\" in the dirt. 

If you are seeing shadows that look like they belong to the texture's shape but appear even with uniform colors, those are **actual lighting calculations**. If the geometry is very complex or if there are overlapping parts of the mesh (self-shadowing), the engine might be casting internal shadows into the \"pits\" of the stucco.

### [b]How to Test This[/b]
To confirm exactly which one it is, you can try these two tests:

1.  **To test if it's a Roughness issue:** Set the `ROUGHNESS` value in your material to a fixed number (like 0.5) or even turn off the dynamic calculation. If the blotches stay, it's not a roughness problem.
2.  **To check for lighting/shadows:** Temporarily turn off **Shadows** and **Ambient Occlusion** in your project settings (or just on this specific material). If the blotches disappear or change significantly, then the \"darkness\" you see is actually real-time shadows being cast into the pits of the stucco.

